  "findings": [
    {
      "description": "The code executes irreversible DROP COLUMN migrations against the production database (hardcoded PRODUCTION_DB) with no dry-run, backup, confirmation, or rollback mechanism. This can cause permanent data loss. (file: bm-10_l2_rollback.py, lines 1-12)",
      "category": "data-loss",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "description": "cleanup_files() recursively deletes an entire backup directory via shutil.rmtree based only on BACKUP_DIR env without path validation or confirmation; if misconfigured it can delete unrelated data. (file: bm-10_l2_rollback.py, lines 17-22)",
      "category": "data-loss",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "description": "Production database connection URL is hardcoded in source code, making environment-specific configuration and safe rotation difficult. Hardcoded infrastructure endpoints should not be committed.",
      "category": "other",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "description": "There is no transactional safety or error handling around the migration: the drop statements are executed sequentially and committed as one batch without a backup or verification step. A failed/partial migration can leave production in an inconsistent state.",
      "category": "logic-bug",
      "severity": "high"
    }
  ],
  "decision": "REJECT",
  "fix_description": "Recommended fixes (all are L2 high-risk and require human approval before execution; no automatic patch applied): 1) Remove the hardcoded PRODUCTION_DB constant and load the connection string from a secure environment variable/config secret; never commit production endpoints or credentials. 2) Make drop_legacy_columns safe: add a dry-run mode, require an explicit --confirm/--dangerous-ack flag, create and verify a backup before running, wrap the migration in a transaction with rollback on failure, validate that the target tables/columns exist, and add error handling/logging. 3) Make cleanup_files safe: validate the backup path against an expected prefix, require explicit confirmation, and avoid shutil.rmtree by moving files to a quarantine/trash location or using a safer file-by-file deletion with checks. 4) Add comprehensive tests and a rollback plan before the migration is approved.",
